1
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:11,950
Greetings and welcome to this lecture module on performance.

2
00:00:13,470 --> 00:00:20,350
We have now completed the cycle of risk assessment, risk mitigation and performance assessment.

3
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:24,670
In this lecture module, we will focus on performance assessment.

4
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:31,710
As an introduction, biorisk performance pertains to improving biorisk management by recording,

5
00:00:32,350 --> 00:00:38,350
measuring and evaluating organizational actions and outcomes to reduce biorisk.

6
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:44,990
You may recall that in the first lecture on the laboratory biorisk management system,

7
00:00:44,990 --> 00:00:49,230
I introduced you to the concept of the biorisk management policy.

8
00:00:49,790 --> 00:00:57,150
This policy is then translated into specific goals and objectives and setting goals

9
00:00:58,110 --> 00:01:04,910
and ensuring that we comply with specific criteria in order to meet these goals is an essential

10
00:01:04,910 --> 00:01:16,740
component of laboratory biorisk management. The objectives of this module are to introduce you

11
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:22,900
to the concept of performance assessment, to introduce you to the concept of setting goals

12
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:34,300
and to teach you how to measure performance in a laboratory setting. The learning outcomes

13
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:39,180
for this particular module are as follows. Upon completion of this module, you should be able to

14
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:46,220
establish goals, assess performance based on attainment of goals and improve performance of

15
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:59,940
existing processes or objectives. Let us recap the AMP model which has formed the basis for this

16
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:08,100
particular MOOC. So as I mentioned earlier, the AMP model is based on risk assessment,

17
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:16,260
risk mitigation and performance assessment. So you first assess the risk posed by

18
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:23,460
a specific operation or process involving a biological agent. You then mitigate the risk

19
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:31,620
using appropriate controls and when you mitigate the risk you have what is known as residual risk.

20
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:40,580
You have to decide whether you want to proceed to work with this inherent residual risk and then

21
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:47,940
you have to assess the performance of your specific procedures or objectives by conducting

22
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:55,300
an audit and this all constitutes the process of continuous quality improvement in a laboratory

23
00:02:55,300 --> 00:03:10,050
biarisk management system. Let us now move on to performance. The concept of biarisk

24
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:17,250
performance is based on the way in which a biarisk management system functions to manage

25
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:25,330
or minimize biarisk. As biarisk managers, we look at the system in its entirety.

26
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:33,570
We do not merely look at the biological agent itself and the risks associated with it. We also

27
00:03:33,570 --> 00:03:40,210
look at other factors which include human factors as well as logistical factors and

28
00:03:40,210 --> 00:03:46,770
factors related to infrastructure of the particular containment facility. So the concept

29
00:03:46,770 --> 00:03:57,580
of performance encompasses all of these factors. Performance actually measures the difference

30
00:03:57,580 --> 00:04:03,100
between your intended outcomes and your observed outcomes. Take for instance a very simple example

31
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:11,340
you have decided to run a race of one kilometer and you assume that you can complete this race

32
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:19,820
in 15 minutes. However when you actually undertake this race you require 20 minutes to complete

33
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:26,700
the one kilometer race. Now there may be multiple factors which you have not taken into consideration

34
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:32,700
prior to implementing this particular objective and these may include the weather, your level of

35
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:39,100
fitness as well as your access to equipment and when you conduct a performance assessment

36
00:04:39,740 --> 00:04:45,340
you try and determine these factors which undermine your performance and that is what

37
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:51,260
performance is all about. It's identifying those factors which undermine performance

38
00:04:52,220 --> 00:05:04,220
and which deviate you from your intended outcomes. Let us look at this example. For instance at the

39
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:10,380
beginning of the year you have decided that your intended outcomes in your laboratory biarisk

40
00:05:10,380 --> 00:05:19,580
management system should comply with the following indicators. The number of incidents

41
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:25,020
at your respective facility over a period of one year should be less than five. The number of

42
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:29,740
accidents should be less than three and the number of spills in the laboratory should be zero.

43
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:36,780
So you complete your observation over a period of one year and you check your records and what

44
00:05:36,780 --> 00:05:43,580
you actually observe is that the number of incidents is six which is greater than five.

45
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:50,220
The number of accidents is less than three but you actually observe there were no accidents

46
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:56,940
and the number of spills was one. Now based on this you can take necessary corrective action.

47
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:04,220
For instance the number of incidents indicate that the SOP may not be

48
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:12,620
integral to the functioning of that particular process so a revision of the SOP may be in order.

49
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:19,820
An incident may also indicate a lack of training and training of personnel may be required

50
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:25,420
as a corrective action. The number of accidents are zero so there is no corrective action required

51
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:32,220
and there's a spill in the laboratory which can be considered as an incident and this may

52
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:39,100
require revision of SOP. So each incident and accident must be investigated and the necessary

53
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:47,500
corrective action must be taken. This is an example of how we set goals for intended outcomes

54
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:59,340
and observe the actual outcomes in laboratory operations. The bi-risk management system is

55
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:06,940
basically evaluating performance based on all of the activities and efforts of people

56
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:13,740
in the facility. So this is an integral assessment. The actual bi-risk management

57
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:18,620
system performance may not match the planned level of risk management effectiveness

58
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:24,140
and performance measurement involves assessing the difference between the

59
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:33,900
objectives and the observed outcomes. Performance may also change over a period of time based on the

60
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:44,530
level of performance which requires continual effort. The concept of performance is based on

61
00:07:44,530 --> 00:07:52,530
an input, the steps related to an input and an output. For instance if your input is a process

62
00:07:53,170 --> 00:08:00,130
to diagnose a specific sample there will be specific steps associated with the diagnosis

63
00:08:00,130 --> 00:08:06,210
of a biological agent in a particular sample. There will also be an output associated with

64
00:08:06,210 --> 00:08:13,890
these steps and performance can be measured by maintaining documentation at each stage of

65
00:08:13,890 --> 00:08:24,670
this particular process. When you consider performance and setting of goals you must first

66
00:08:25,630 --> 00:08:31,950
raise these relevant questions. Are the steps physically possible? Are they the right steps?

67
00:08:32,910 --> 00:08:38,990
Is the actual output equal to the desired output and is the output the right measure of the

68
00:08:38,990 --> 00:08:46,750
process? These are the various questions which you must ask yourself and your team when you

69
00:08:46,750 --> 00:08:57,330
design an assessment for performance. Let us look at this example. The input is to mitigate

70
00:08:57,330 --> 00:09:03,010
the risk of exposure of laboratory workers to mycobacterium tuberculosis using appropriate

71
00:09:03,010 --> 00:09:09,090
administrative controls and personal protective equipment. Now this is an example of a lab

72
00:09:09,090 --> 00:09:16,210
involved in the diagnosis of mycobacterium tuberculosis. So that particular lab has decided

73
00:09:16,210 --> 00:09:22,050
to implement specific controls which include administrative controls and PPEs.

74
00:09:22,690 --> 00:09:29,090
The steps they have introduced are basically the first one is to conduct a preliminary health

75
00:09:29,090 --> 00:09:36,210
screening of laboratory workers to determine that they are not infected by mycobacterium

76
00:09:36,210 --> 00:09:43,570
tuberculosis prior to working in this particular facility. A standard operating procedure has been

77
00:09:43,570 --> 00:09:50,050
put into place for the appropriate use of PPEs at this facility and post-project screening of

78
00:09:50,050 --> 00:09:55,810
laboratories has also been incorporated into the standard operating procedure for this particular

79
00:09:55,810 --> 00:10:01,890
facility. However after completion of this particular experiment it is observed that one

80
00:10:01,890 --> 00:10:09,810
of the five laboratory workers at the facility has tested positive for mycobacterium tuberculosis.

81
00:10:10,370 --> 00:10:17,970
Now this raises a question to the virus manager. The virus manager will have to question whether

82
00:10:17,970 --> 00:10:27,570
the SOP is appropriate and if this SOP is not appropriate additional measures for improvement

83
00:10:27,570 --> 00:10:35,410
of this SOP will have to be taken into account and an accident analysis will have to be conducted.

84
00:10:36,210 --> 00:10:42,210
This is an example of how a goal has been set and how the actual observation is different

85
00:10:42,210 --> 00:10:52,770
from the intended goal. Performance assessment is an essential component of the BRM

86
00:10:54,130 --> 00:11:02,130
because it actually supports existing benchmarks and we can identify which components meet the

87
00:11:02,130 --> 00:11:08,370
benchmarks and which do not and if they do not you can ask why don't they meet the benchmark.

88
00:11:08,370 --> 00:11:16,770
For example in the case of the TB one of the workers was exposed to the TB so we then proceed

89
00:11:16,770 --> 00:11:23,810
to asking ourselves the question why and the question must be answered and supported by

90
00:11:23,810 --> 00:11:33,500
appropriate documentation and this is where monitoring comes into play. Performance assessment

91
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:39,500
may also be required for certification and accreditation as an auditor who is involved

92
00:11:39,580 --> 00:11:44,540
in the accreditation or certification of your lab will definitely demand evidence of records.

93
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:51,980
It prevents accidents and incidents as all of the operations within a laboratory are considered

94
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:58,620
within the scope of specific standard operating procedures and it reduces the cost of operations.

95
00:12:06,020 --> 00:12:13,580
H. James Harrington has stated this with regard to measuring performance and I quote

96
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:21,180
measurement is the first step that leads to control and eventually to improvement. If you can't measure

97
00:12:21,180 --> 00:12:28,060
something you can't understand it. If you can't understand it you can't control it. If you can't

98
00:12:28,060 --> 00:12:37,420
control it you can't improve it. Now this is basically the definition or the concept which

99
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:45,020
drives all elements of performance. You must measure performance as the first step and you

100
00:12:45,020 --> 00:12:51,660
must measure it using appropriate tools or metrics. In the case of surveys we call these

101
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:59,420
tools instruments which measure performance. Once you measure it you can understand it

102
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:07,100
and you can interpret the results of your measurement and once you understand it

103
00:13:07,100 --> 00:13:13,340
you can control it. If you can't control it you basically cannot improve performance. So this

104
00:13:13,340 --> 00:13:26,260
involves the structured approach towards measuring performance. So there are challenges with measuring

105
00:13:26,260 --> 00:13:33,540
performance which basically focuses on what went wrong and post incident and accident analysis.

106
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:42,340
Now a good BiRisk manager must be able to preempt incidents and accidents by observing behavior

107
00:13:43,220 --> 00:13:50,260
and this can come through experience or through interaction with other BiRisk managers who have

108
00:13:50,260 --> 00:13:56,340
experience at different facilities and who have encountered incidents and accidents during the

109
00:13:56,340 --> 00:14:06,740
course of their career. The first point to note is that BiRisk performance management is an integral

110
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:13,140
part of the overall BiRisk management system. It pertains to standard operating procedures as well as

111
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:26,160
actual operations at the facility when implementing specific processes. Now the CWA document clearly

112
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:32,880
defines performance management and analysis. It states that organizations shall ensure that

113
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:40,400
appropriate data are determined collected and analyzed to assess the suitability and effectiveness

114
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:45,680
of the BiRisk management system and to evaluate where continual improvement of the system can

115
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:53,280
be made. Now in the current scenario we have big data analytics. For instance you can install

116
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:59,840
multiple closed circuit TVs in your respective facilities and you can also

117
00:14:59,840 --> 00:15:07,040
link these to artificial intelligence applications and you can basically collect

118
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:13,600
very large sets of data which can be analyzed later and this basically reduces the cost of

119
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:22,320
collecting data using human interfaces. So an increased trend to use big data analytics

120
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:27,920
is generally considered an advantage to laboratory BiRisk management systems.

121
00:15:32,180 --> 00:15:38,260
The CWA also states that the management shall review the organization's BiRisk management system

122
00:15:38,260 --> 00:15:44,900
at planned intervals to ensure its continuing suitability, adequacy and effectiveness. Now the

123
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:53,780
planned intervals can be on a periodic basis or can be done annually and these intervals must be

124
00:15:53,780 --> 00:16:00,420
synchronized with your protocols or your procedures for certification and validation of your

125
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:07,140
respective protocols. The review includes assessing opportunities for improvement and the

126
00:16:07,140 --> 00:16:14,020
need for changes to the system, procedures, policies and objectives. So this is enshrined in the CWA

127
00:16:14,020 --> 00:16:22,610
document. We now move on to planning for performance assessment and planning for

128
00:16:22,610 --> 00:16:28,290
performance assessment answers many questions before undertaking measurements and analysis

129
00:16:28,850 --> 00:16:34,850
and ensuring that you focus on the most critical elements of assessment. Before you conduct a

130
00:16:34,850 --> 00:16:42,130
performance assessment you must devise an instrument for review of performance and this can be in

131
00:16:42,130 --> 00:16:47,410
the form of an audit which is conducted via a questionnaire. It can be in the form of a bench

132
00:16:47,410 --> 00:16:54,450
audit in which case the BiRisk manager follows a researcher as he or she undertakes a specific

133
00:16:55,010 --> 00:17:08,350
SOP or executes a specific procedure. So how does this actually work at a laboratory or

134
00:17:08,350 --> 00:17:15,630
containment facility? We always begin with the policy. Now the policy establishes commitment

135
00:17:15,630 --> 00:17:22,590
and intent. For instance, I have given you an example of a general policy statement.

136
00:17:22,590 --> 00:17:30,910
The organization is committed to implementation of biosafety measures in order to ensure

137
00:17:31,630 --> 00:17:39,150
a safe working environment for the laboratory workers and for the environment. This is a policy.

138
00:17:39,150 --> 00:17:47,310
However, as you notice, this policy does not specify how to implement and this policy must

139
00:17:47,310 --> 00:17:54,350
be translated into roles and responsibilities. Specific goals must be stated and specific

140
00:17:54,350 --> 00:18:06,690
objectives must be stated or developed in order to accomplish these goals. Our goal is an

141
00:18:06,690 --> 00:18:14,450
observable and measurable end result. As in the case of the example with the

142
00:18:14,530 --> 00:18:20,850
Mycobacterium tuberculosis, the goal was to have zero infections of laboratory workers

143
00:18:20,850 --> 00:18:27,170
upon completion of that particular experiment. However, we had one laboratory worker who was

144
00:18:27,170 --> 00:18:34,850
infected. So we have not achieved the goal or the intended outcome and we have to move on

145
00:18:34,850 --> 00:18:45,420
into analysis of why that goal could not be achieved. In order to achieve goals,

146
00:18:46,060 --> 00:18:54,060
we need to undertake specific steps and these steps is what forms the basis for objectives.

147
00:18:54,060 --> 00:19:00,220
So objectives are the steps which we need to take within a fixed time frame to move towards

148
00:19:00,220 --> 00:19:07,820
and achieve the goal. And the best example of an objective and the steps incorporated within it

149
00:19:07,820 --> 00:19:19,020
is the standard operating procedure. When you are a biorisk manager at a facility,

150
00:19:19,100 --> 00:19:27,900
please ensure that the objectives are smart and this translates into specific, measurable, attainable,

151
00:19:28,540 --> 00:19:35,500
reasonable and time days. An objective should always be specific. It should not include too

152
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:43,180
much description. The objective should be measurable as in the case of TB, our objective was to have

153
00:19:43,180 --> 00:19:49,820
zero infections upon completion of the experiment. However, we have one infected laboratory workers,

154
00:19:49,820 --> 00:19:54,220
so we can measure this, we can quantify this. The objective should be attainable

155
00:19:55,100 --> 00:20:01,580
and reasonable. Please do not make objectives which are beyond the scope of your specific

156
00:20:01,580 --> 00:20:07,420
laboratory biorisk management system as they will be very hard to attain and these will be

157
00:20:07,420 --> 00:20:13,660
flagged during an audit. And finally, the objective should be time-based. The objective

158
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:26,510
must be performed or attained within a reasonable time frame. Now, we have to move on to a concept

159
00:20:26,510 --> 00:20:33,150
known as metrics or measures. Just as you measure distance in terms of meters or kilometers or

160
00:20:33,150 --> 00:20:40,910
time in terms of hours and minutes, specific metrics must be developed in order to measure

161
00:20:41,630 --> 00:20:51,260
biorisk management. These should be observable and you can ask this question how many times or how

162
00:20:51,260 --> 00:20:58,700
much. They should be applied regularly and consistently. They should be used to trace

163
00:20:58,700 --> 00:21:03,980
deviations from expected performance and they should provide a warning before an incident.

164
00:21:04,860 --> 00:21:10,940
Now, the measures which we generally use at a biorisk management facility are related to

165
00:21:10,940 --> 00:21:19,180
incidents and accidents. So, if we decide that we have an objective to have zero incidents or our

166
00:21:19,180 --> 00:21:26,140
goal is to have zero incidents at the end of the year, we must assess the number of incidents or

167
00:21:26,140 --> 00:21:34,380
accidents and relate these to the policy, the goals and the objectives. So, if we do not achieve

168
00:21:34,380 --> 00:21:41,820
our goals, we generally conduct an analysis and look back as to the reasons as to why we could

169
00:21:41,820 --> 00:21:50,940
not achieve those specific goals. There are leading and lagging performance indicators.

170
00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:56,780
Leading performance indicators are related to processes, culture, behavior and protective

171
00:21:56,780 --> 00:22:02,220
barriers. So, leading performance indicators are those indicators which you define as a

172
00:22:02,220 --> 00:22:09,980
biorisk manager and these basically set the agenda for performance at your facility.

173
00:22:12,540 --> 00:22:19,900
We also have lagging indicators and these lagging indicators are indicators which you observe after

174
00:22:19,900 --> 00:22:27,180
a particular incident has taken place. For instance, an accident or a near miss is an

175
00:22:27,180 --> 00:22:32,940
indication of a lagging indicator. A lagging indicator basically means that you did not

176
00:22:32,940 --> 00:22:40,300
foresee this particular incident or accident and it occurred. O-enforcement actions can be also an

177
00:22:40,300 --> 00:22:47,020
indication of a lagging indicator. You have enforced a specific action because you observed

178
00:22:47,740 --> 00:22:52,460
that there was an accident or incident. So, you change the standard operating procedure

179
00:22:52,460 --> 00:22:57,740
and you enforce a specific set of rules or guidelines on the laboratory personnel.

180
00:22:57,740 --> 00:23:09,010
So, these are indications of lagging indicators. Ideal indicators are relevant to the organization.

181
00:23:09,570 --> 00:23:15,250
They are objective and practical. They are immediate and reliable. They are cost-effective.

182
00:23:16,290 --> 00:23:21,090
There's a sense of ownership because these indicators are developed as a joint

183
00:23:21,090 --> 00:23:26,610
consultative process with individuals across the organization. They guide future corrective

184
00:23:26,610 --> 00:23:38,690
action and they provide opportunities for improvement. The ideal indicator should be

185
00:23:38,690 --> 00:23:45,730
selected based on what needs to be measured. For instance, the number of accidents is an

186
00:23:45,730 --> 00:23:55,090
indicator which implies or determines the level of compliance with existing standard operating

187
00:23:55,170 --> 00:24:01,570
procedures at your respective facility. Indicators must be quantifiable.

188
00:24:02,610 --> 00:24:08,290
Indicators should be able to measure the performance priorities. It should examine all

189
00:24:08,290 --> 00:24:14,290
the levels of laboratory virus management adaptable to different situations and indicators

190
00:24:14,290 --> 00:24:24,020
should not be selected based on ease of management. An example of a safety performance

191
00:24:24,020 --> 00:24:29,300
indicator is the extent to which procedures established in the safety management system

192
00:24:29,300 --> 00:24:36,740
are applied by employees. Okay, this is a very clear example in the case of a fire safety drill.

193
00:24:37,700 --> 00:24:42,900
So, generally a fire safety drill or an evacuation drill is conducted at facilities

194
00:24:43,620 --> 00:24:52,580
in order to prepare for an emergency such as a fire involving chemical or electrical agents.

195
00:24:53,460 --> 00:24:59,300
So, in the case of a fire safety drill, the auditor will generally ask for records of

196
00:25:00,420 --> 00:25:06,420
a drill. So, this involves documentation of the drill and this will give you an evidence of

197
00:25:06,420 --> 00:25:16,100
training. Safety performance indicators also focus on the appropriate PPEs which may be available

198
00:25:16,100 --> 00:25:24,100
to manage risks associated with biological agents. The frequency and scope of audits are

199
00:25:24,100 --> 00:25:31,460
also examples of safety indicators and alignment to current standards, codes of practice and guidelines

200
00:25:31,460 --> 00:25:42,700
is under indication and you may have specific indicators for the facility. Now, indicators

201
00:25:42,700 --> 00:25:48,780
must be used judiciously. The metrics must be established by the committee prior to the

202
00:25:48,780 --> 00:25:54,780
deployment of the indicator. Indicators are based on methods of data collection and the

203
00:25:54,780 --> 00:26:01,580
available information and the data must be presented in a format that is legible or can

204
00:26:01,580 --> 00:26:08,700
be understood by the laboratory management as well as all the workers associated with the facility

205
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:13,500
and data must also be presented based on who will use that particular data.

206
00:26:19,150 --> 00:26:24,910
Data can be collected using multiple methods. However, it must be collected within a specific

207
00:26:24,910 --> 00:26:32,670
time frame and the data must be able to measure the level of performance and should take into

208
00:26:32,670 --> 00:26:39,070
account the methods, the cost and as well as intrusive factors. For instance, we are

209
00:26:39,070 --> 00:26:45,310
collecting data related to standard operating procedures and this involves the bench audit.

210
00:26:45,310 --> 00:26:52,510
The bench audit in itself is intrusive as the person being audited becomes self-conscious

211
00:26:53,470 --> 00:27:00,590
and this adds to intrusiveness and this can contribute to a breach of containment

212
00:27:00,590 --> 00:27:09,150
or a violation of a specific protocol. So, data collection should be as least

213
00:27:09,150 --> 00:27:20,660
intrusive as possible and it should not disrupt normal operations. Upon completion of data

214
00:27:20,660 --> 00:27:28,580
analysis, a laboratory manager generally moves on to analysis and interpretation of the data

215
00:27:28,580 --> 00:27:35,220
and review of performance. Now, this process involves time and resources and it must be

216
00:27:35,220 --> 00:27:41,700
done by dedicated personnel within a designated time frame. What this means is that the

217
00:27:41,700 --> 00:27:49,300
management must allocate a specific individual or a group of individuals to conduct performance

218
00:27:49,380 --> 00:27:57,300
analysis and to give them a designated time frame. Let him focus on one specific task before moving

219
00:27:57,300 --> 00:28:06,580
on to another one in order to ensure the integrity of performance analysis. The reason

220
00:28:06,580 --> 00:28:15,060
or the objective behind PDCA and AMP is eventually continual improvement. However,

221
00:28:15,060 --> 00:28:20,100
the performance management system itself must be reviewed periodically because laboratory

222
00:28:20,100 --> 00:28:27,220
management systems evolve with time. The management may have new priorities or the

223
00:28:27,220 --> 00:28:33,300
facility itself may be modified to cater to new diagnostic procedures or research objectives.

224
00:28:34,260 --> 00:28:40,660
This is when you as a BiRisk Manager must decide whether the measures are relevant to your

225
00:28:40,660 --> 00:28:47,540
existing or current VRM priorities. You should also decide whether the measures provide performance

226
00:28:47,540 --> 00:28:54,340
insights, if new measures are required, and if all of the measures assist in achieving the

227
00:28:54,340 --> 00:29:01,460
institutional goals. In many cases, we adopt measures from other containment facilities or

228
00:29:01,460 --> 00:29:06,740
other laboratory management systems and these may not be pertinent to our respective situations.

229
00:29:06,740 --> 00:29:13,620
And this is where the wisdom of a BiRisk Management system or basically a BiRisk Manager

230
00:29:13,620 --> 00:29:22,740
is wise enough to view and revise the performance management system in order to cater to evolving

231
00:29:23,860 --> 00:29:35,440
systems. We have now come to the end of this module and performance assessment is basically the

232
00:29:36,160 --> 00:29:41,760
third step in the AMP process, risk assessment, risk mitigation, and finally we have performance

233
00:29:41,760 --> 00:29:47,520
assessment. And performance assessment is critical to the establishment of a sound laboratory BiRisk

234
00:29:47,520 --> 00:29:53,120
Management system. You cannot improve the system if you do not assess the performance of that

235
00:29:53,120 --> 00:30:01,920
particular system. Performance assessment also helps you to measure whether your policies and goals

236
00:30:01,920 --> 00:30:08,560
are being translated into specific objectives. It established metrics for the measurement of

237
00:30:08,560 --> 00:30:15,280
performance and it also provides you the basis for investigating accidents and incidents.

238
00:30:17,840 --> 00:30:25,040
We can take necessary corrective action when we measure the performance of a system. Without

239
00:30:25,040 --> 00:30:31,280
measurement, we cannot have corrective action and we can also monitor the impact of corrective

240
00:30:31,280 --> 00:30:41,040
actions using performance. So performance is a very useful method to convince your laboratory

241
00:30:41,040 --> 00:30:47,680
managers or your top management to improve the system. That brings us to the end of this module

242
00:30:47,680 --> 00:30:53,280
on performance assessment. Thank you very much for participating in this module and I wish you

243
00:30:53,280 --> 00:31:02,640
a pleasant learning experience. Thank you.
